card.statement:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be any subset of $\mathcal{R}$. Given $(i,j),\ (r,s)\in \mathfrak{V}(\mathcal{C})$ we will write:

- [(i)] $(i,j)\succeq_{\mathcal{C}} (r,s)$ if, there exists $\{(i_{1},j_{1}),\ldots,(i_{m},j_{m})\}\subseteq \mathfrak{V}(\mathcal{C})$ such that

\begin{align}
\{(i,j)\geq (i_{1},j_1),\  (i_{1},j_1)\geq (i_2,j_2), \cdots,\  (i_{m},j_m)\geq (r,s)\}&\subseteq \mathcal{C}
\end{align}

- [(ii)] We write $(i,j)\succ_{\mathcal{C}} (r,s)$ if there exists $\{(i_{1},j_{1}),\ldots,(i_{m},j_{m})\}\subseteq \mathfrak{V}(\mathcal{C})$ such that in the condition <<ref:geq sub C>>, at least one of the inequalities is $>$.

Given another set of relations $\mathcal{C}'$, we say that $\mathcal{C}$ implies $\mathcal{C}'$ if whenever we have $(i,j)\succ_{\mathcal{C}'} (r,s)$ (respectively $(i,j)\succeq_{\mathcal{C}'} (r,s)$) we also have $(i,j)\succ_{\mathcal{C}} (r,s)$ (respectively $(i,j)\succeq_{\mathcal{C}} (r,s)$).
